# Transmutation 002

## Build It Anyway

*Discovered October 1, 2026*

---

## Origin

**Project** Nagare

I originally built Nagare as a personal task management application to organize and track my work.

On October 1, I started using it again.

There are already countless task management applications, some of which are far more sophisticated than Nagare. Mine isn't particularly novel or groundbreaking. It is a relatively simple application that does what I need it to do.

But I use it regularly, and it has genuinely helped me organize my work.

That made me reconsider something I had often done when coming up with personal project ideas: dismissing them because something similar already existed.

---

## Price Paid

* Hesitating over ideas because similar products already existed.
* Overthinking whether an idea was sufficiently original to be worth building.
* Dismissing projects that could have been useful simply because they weren't particularly innovative.
* Spending more time questioning an idea's worth than exploring what I could learn or gain from building it.

---

## Observation

Nagare wasn't a new concept. Task management applications have existed for years, and there are plenty of alternatives available.

But none of that stopped Nagare from being useful to me.

I had built something relatively simple, started using it again, and found that it genuinely helped me.

It didn't need to compete with existing products or offer anything revolutionary. It just needed to work for me.

I realized that I had been unnecessarily applying a different standard to my personal projects. I was evaluating ideas based on how original they were instead of considering whether I actually wanted to build them.

---

## Root Cause

I was confusing originality with value.

I had started associating the worth of a project with how different it was from existing solutions.

That made me overlook other reasons to build something.

A project could be useful to me, give me an opportunity to explore a technology, let me experiment with an implementation, or simply be something I wanted to bring to life.

None of those reasons required the idea to be new.

I was treating the existence of an alternative as a reason to reconsider an idea, when it should simply have been another factor to consider.

---

## Truth Obtained

**Something doesn't have to be new to be worth building.**

The existence of an existing solution doesn't invalidate my idea.

If I want to build a notes application, a task manager, an art tracker, or anything else, I don't need to justify it by making it revolutionary.

It can be a familiar concept, a simpler implementation, or even something that already has hundreds of alternatives.

If I want to build it, learn from it, and eventually use it, that is reason enough.

Not every personal project needs to become a product, solve a completely new problem, or be impressive to someone else.

Sometimes, I just want something to exist, and I can build it myself.

---

## Equivalent Exchange

### When an idea comes up

Don't dismiss it just because something similar already exists.

* Look at existing alternatives, but don't let their existence discourage me.
* Think about what I want to build and why I want to build it.
* Consider whether it would be useful to me, interesting to work on, or a good learning opportunity.

### While evaluating the idea

* Don't manufacture novelty just to make the project seem worthwhile.
* Don't compare a small personal project against mature products with years of development behind them.
* Keep the scope appropriate for what I actually want to achieve.
* Remember that I am not necessarily building something for a market or an audience.

### During implementation

Build because I want to.

* Don't overthink whether the idea is impressive enough.
* Don't turn every personal project into a potential product.
* Focus on making something functional that I actually want to use.
* Allow myself to experiment without expecting every project to become something extraordinary.

The objective isn't to reinvent everything.

It's to stop talking myself out of building things I want to build.

---

## Validation

The next time I hesitate to build something because an alternative already exists, ask myself one question.

**Do I still want to build it?**

If yes, build it.

The existence of another solution doesn't need to be a reason to stop.

---

> *Someone has probably built it before. Build it anyway.*
